WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.220
Most of Srinivasa Ramanujan’s knowledge&nbsp;
came from a single mathematics book.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.580
He spent all of his time thinking&nbsp;
about math and little else.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.460
It's no wonder he flunked out of college - twice…

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.760
Yet somehow, he found his way&nbsp;
to the University of Cambridge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.300
where he performed ground-breaking&nbsp;
research on mathematical problems.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.560
Ramanujan was born on December 22, 1887,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:23.060
in the town of Erode, south of Madras&nbsp;
(present-day Chennai) in South India.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.960
Ramanujan was born on December 22,&nbsp;
1887 in Erode, a town south of Madras,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.000
what's now called Chennai in South India.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.480
He was considered a miracle&nbsp;
child…the only one of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.000
mother’s first four children to survive infancy.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.540
He also survived an outbreak of smallpox. Poor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.500
health would afflict him his whole life&nbsp;
yet it never slowed down his passion.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.700
Math equations danced in his head&nbsp;
as if they appeared from thin air.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.180
He believed his gifts came from&nbsp;
the Hindu goddess Namagiri.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.180
He used to go to the nearby Sarangapani "Temple as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:55.740
a boy to sketch complex mathematical&nbsp;
equations in chalk on stone slabs.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:01:00.060
As a teen, he got his hands on a copy&nbsp;
of a book of math theorems written by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.780
British mathematician George Carr which was&nbsp;
intended as a teaching aid for students.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:07.680
However, many students found it&nbsp;
tough to read because it listed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.900
answers without showing the steps to get there.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.920
But for Ramanujan, this book&nbsp;
awakened the genius in him.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.960
Carr’s book was “like a crossword puzzle,&nbsp;
with its empty grids begging to be filled in”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.860
as Robert Kanigel describes&nbsp;
in his biography on Ramanujan.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:27.600
He had a natural intuition for math which&nbsp;
can be illustrated by this problem he solved.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.760
Imagine you're on a street with 50 to 500&nbsp;
houses. You're looking for a special house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.660
where the sum of all house numbers to&nbsp;
its left equals the sum to its right.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.100
Can you find it?

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:43.800
This actually happened on a street with 288&nbsp;
houses. The special house was number 204,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.200
with both sides totaling 20,706.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:52.260
When the Indian statistician, P. C.&nbsp;
Mahalanobis asked Ramanujan about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:56.640
this problem that he read in a magazine,&nbsp;
Ramanujan thought for a moment and gave a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.840
formula that works for any number of&nbsp;
houses, not just between 50 and 500.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:07.560
On a street with just 8 houses, house 6&nbsp;
is special because 1+2+3+4+5 equals 7+8.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.460
Ramanujan said he knew the&nbsp;
answer was a continued fraction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.940
showing his unique ability to see&nbsp;
patterns that others would miss.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.320
He received a scholarship to study at&nbsp;
the reputable Government Arts College&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:21.300
in his hometown.
of Kumbakonam.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.120
However, his obsession with math got in the way.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.340
He flunked his English composition paper, lost&nbsp;
his scholarship, and dropped out because his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:34.800
family couldn’t afford the 32 rupee tuition per&nbsp;
term, a substantial amount of money at the time.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.840
His father worked as a clerk in a sari shop&nbsp;
and never made more than 20 rupees a month.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.740
Ramanujan felt humiliated and ran away from home.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.720
He gave university another shot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:50.220
but failed the entrance exams administered&nbsp;
by the University of Madras again and again.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.420
He scored less than 10% on the physiology exam.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.840
A former student whom he tutored in&nbsp;
math recalled his state of remind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.880
“...he used to bemoan his wretched conditions&nbsp;
in life…he would reply that many a great man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.980
like Galileo died in inquisition and&nbsp;
his lot would be to die in poverty. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.940
I continued to encourage him that God,&nbsp;
who is great, would surely help him…”

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.580
His failures turned out to be a blessing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:18.120
disguise because now he could&nbsp;
focus on his one true passion.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.800
He educated himself with that outdated&nbsp;
math book and began feverishly stuffing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.660
his notebooks with new formulas of his&nbsp;
own, totaling nearly 4,000 in his lifetime.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:32.280
He started to build a reputation after&nbsp;
publishing his work in the first academic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.480
journal dedicated to mathematics in&nbsp;
India, the Indian Mathematical Society.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.300
But it wasn’t long before he had to find a job.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.960
At the age of 21, Ramanujan's parents arranged his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.940
marriage to nine-year-old Janaki a distant&nbsp;
relative. Such customs were common then.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:51.360
He began working as an accounting&nbsp;
clerk at the Port of Madras,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.360
a big shipping hub known&nbsp;
today as the Port of Chennai.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.200
As luck would have it, his direct&nbsp;
supervisor happened to be a mathematician,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.600
and the head of the port was a British engineer.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:04.320
They encouraged him to write to English&nbsp;
mathematicians about his discoveries.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:07.680
But two esteemed Cambridge&nbsp;
mathematicians rejected him.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.260
However, a third was intrigued.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:15.900
In January 1913, G.H. Hardy, a&nbsp;
fellow of Trinity College at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.560
University of Cambridge, received&nbsp;
a letter from Ramanujan that read:

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:20.580
Dear Sir,&nbsp;

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:26.760
I beg to introduce myself to you as a clerk in&nbsp;
the Accounts Department of the Port Trust Office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:34.620
at Madras on a salary of only £20 per annum.&nbsp;
I am now about 23 years of age. I have had no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:39.900
University education…After leaving school&nbsp;
I have been employing the spare time at my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:44.940
disposal to work at Mathematics. I have made&nbsp;
a special investigation of divergent series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:49.860
in general and the results I get are termed&nbsp;
by the local mathematicians as "startling".

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:54.300
The ‘startling’ claim is that adding&nbsp;
up all positive integers, 1, 2, 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:58.200
4, and so on, to infinity added up to -1/12.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.480
Bear in mind, this isn't your typical addition.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:04.080
Divergent series emerge from a&nbsp;
complex mathematical process.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:06.900
Ramanujan’s letter was ten pages long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:10.560
consisting mostly of technical results&nbsp;
from a wide range of mathematics.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.080
He claimed to have a technique for counting prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:17.340
numbers - numbers greater than 1 that&nbsp;
can only be divided by 1 and by itself.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.160
Prime numbers are notoriously unpredictable.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.160
It’s like trying to understand if there's&nbsp;
a pattern that would somehow help you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.920
know exactly when your favorite&nbsp;
song will be played on the radio.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.180
Although Ramanujan failed to find a&nbsp;
perfect formula for predicting primes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:33.480
his work provided fresh insights&nbsp;
into their distribution.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:37.260
He also included theorems&nbsp;
related to integral calculus.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:41.580
Integral calculus can be likened&nbsp;
to slicing a sausage and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.220
reassembling it, making it whole or “integral”.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:47.160
It's a mathematical tool&nbsp;
with real-world applications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:49.800
like determining the drag on a plane's wing.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:55.380
As a plane flies, the air hits the wing in&nbsp;
tiny "slices" over time, each contributing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.380 --> 00:06:00.240
to drag. Integral calculus adds up these&nbsp;
small effects to calculate the total drag.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:03.120
Ramanjuan’s letter to Hardy ended this way:

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:09.420
“Being poor, if you are convinced that there is&nbsp;
anything of value I would like to have my theorems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:15.720
published. Being inexperienced I would very&nbsp;
highly value any advice you give me. Requesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:23.520
to be excused for the trouble I give you. I&nbsp;
remain, Dear Sir, Yours truly, S. Ramanujan.”

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.980
Hardy didn’t know what to make of this unusual&nbsp;
letter from a young man 5,000 miles away.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:33.900
Was this a practical joke? Or, had he&nbsp;
stumbled upon a second Sir Isaac Newton?

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:38.220
Hardy showed the letter to his colleague,&nbsp;
J. E. Littlewood, who was equally amazed.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:43.620
Some of the formulas were familiar, others Hardy&nbsp;
remarked “seemed scarcely possible to believe…”

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:49.080
He concluded that the letter “...was certainly&nbsp;
the most remarkable that I have ever received…”

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.300
Hardy responded to Ramanujan:

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:56.880
“I was exceedingly interested by your letter&nbsp;
and by the theorems which you state. You will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:00.780
however understand that, before I can judge&nbsp;
properly of the value of what you have done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:04.380
it is essential that I should see&nbsp;
proofs of some of your assertions.”

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:08.100
Such proof was required if others&nbsp;
were to be convinced of the results.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:12.240
Ramanujan didn’t bother explaining&nbsp;
how he arrived at his conclusions;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.060
he just lept from insight to insight.

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:18.600
Perhaps he took a page out of&nbsp;
the book that so inspired him.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:24.420
In his response to Hardy’s request to see proof,&nbsp;
Ramanujan mentioned his divergent series result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:32.280
writing: “If I tell you this you will at once&nbsp;
point out to me the lunatic asylum as my goal.”

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:38.220
Ramanujan wanted someone of stature like Hardy to&nbsp;
recognize the worth in his work so that he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:45.360
get a scholarship, since, “I am already a half&nbsp;
starving man. To preserve my brains I want food…”

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.840
Hardy wanted to arrange a scholarship&nbsp;
for him to study at Cambridge.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:53.700
But crossing the ocean was considered a&nbsp;
serious violation of Ramanujan’s devout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:57.060
orthodox Hindu faith that could&nbsp;
lead to losing his caste status.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:01.560
Hardy’s colleague E.H. Neville who&nbsp;
was lecturing in Madras had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:03.960
task of convincing Ramanujan to go to Cambridge.

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:07.740
He assured him his strict vegetarian&nbsp;
diet would be respected in England.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:13.080
Any concerns Ramanujan had disappeared&nbsp;
after his mother had a vivid dream in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:17.160
the Hindu goddess Namagiri instructed&nbsp;
her not to hinder her son's destiny.

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:23.280
On March 17, 1914, Ramanujan set&nbsp;
sail for the journey of his life.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:25.920
He prepared for European life by learning to eat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:28.920
with a knife and fork and&nbsp;
learning how to tie a tie.

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:31.980
After a three-day journey, he&nbsp;
arrived at Trinity College,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:34.980
Cambridge, to start an&nbsp;
extraordinary collaboration.

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:37.560
He and Hardy couldn’t have&nbsp;
been any more different.

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:42.480
Ramanujan was a self-taught savant who believed&nbsp;
equations expressed the thoughts of God.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:45.120
Hardy was a Cambridge professor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.360
an avowed atheist who refused to&nbsp;
believe what he could not prove.

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:50.640
Yet their partnership flourished.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:56.220
Ramanujan and Hardy contributed substantially&nbsp;
to number theory, a branch of mathematics that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.220 --> 00:09:00.000
deals with the fascinating properties and&nbsp;
patterns found within ordinary numbers.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.260
One of their most notable works&nbsp;
calculated the 'partitions' of a number.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:08.040
4 can be partitioned (or&nbsp;
broken down) in five ways.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:12.840
While this may sound straightforward, figuring&nbsp;
out how many ways a number can be partitioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.420
becomes increasingly complex with larger numbers.

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:20.640
The number of partitions of 50 is 204,226.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:24.240
This has practical implications&nbsp;
that aren’t immediately obvious.

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:28.920
Partitions can help computers operate&nbsp;
more efficiently; by dividing tasks or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:33.000
data into smaller parts, devices&nbsp;
can process information quicker.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:36.120
Ramanujan pressed on with his work despite being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:38.520
in poor health for much of&nbsp;
his five years in England.

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:40.440
The colder weather didn’t help.

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:43.260
He was one found shivering in his Cambridge room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:47.640
sleeping atop the blankets,&nbsp;
unaware he should be under them.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:51.960
Maintaining a nutritious vegetarian&nbsp;
diet was also difficult in light of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:53.880
the rationing imposed during World War One.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:59.820
He also skipped meals and ate at random hours of&nbsp;
the day, with no mother or wife to care for him.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:05.220
Ramanujan was eventually diagnosed with&nbsp;
tuberculosis and a severe vitamin deficiency.

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:10.860
Being ill and far away from his family&nbsp;
also affected his mental well-being.

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:13.920
In 1918, he threw himself onto the tracks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:16.560
of the London Underground in&nbsp;
front of an approaching train.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:19.380
Luckily, a guard spotted him and pulled a switch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:22.620
bringing the train to a stop&nbsp;
a few feet before hitting him.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:28.140
His spirits improved considerably later that&nbsp;
year when Britain’s elite body of scientists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:33.720
the Royal Society, named him a Fellow, the&nbsp;
second Indian at the time to be so honored.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:36.600
He was elected partly for his&nbsp;
work on elliptic functions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:39.480
which are used to explain&nbsp;
complex shapes and patterns.

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:43.980
Elliptic functions can accurately describe&nbsp;
the movement of planets around the sun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:47.220
which is neither a perfect&nbsp;
circle nor an exact oval.

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:51.180
It’s like having a super-detailed&nbsp;
map of the movement of planets.

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:54.300
Becoming a fellow of the Royal Society is believed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:57.780
to have stimulated the discovery of&nbsp;
some of his most beautiful theorems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:03.300
which he continued to develop upon returning&nbsp;
to India in 1919…to a hero’s welcome.

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:06.720
His life inspired the movie&nbsp;
The Man Who Knew Infinity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:10.200
reflecting his profound insights&nbsp;
into the nature of infinity.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:17.100
An example is Pi, which starts with 3.14 and&nbsp;
has an infinite number of decimal places. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:20.340
can never write down every single digit&nbsp;
of pi, because there's no end to them!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:25.080
Ramanujan got us closer and closer to&nbsp;
this mysterious number in a faster way.

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:29.280
Back home in India, Ramanujan kept&nbsp;
in touch with Professor Hardy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:34.620
and, in a letter that turned out to be his&nbsp;
last, he hinted at an incredible discovery:

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:35.880
Dear Hardy,

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:41.040
I am extremely sorry for not writing you&nbsp;
a single letter up to now. I discovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:45.000
very interesting functions recently&nbsp;
which I call “Mock” ϑ-functions.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:48.120
Mock theta functions are&nbsp;
a highly abstract concept,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:51.420
like a secret code mathematicians&nbsp;
are still trying to understand.

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:56.700
In 2012, mathematician Ken Ono relied&nbsp;
on Ramanujan’s mock theta functions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.700 --> 00:12:00.660
to devise a new math formula to&nbsp;
better understand black holes.

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:03.720
This approach helps calculate&nbsp;
the entropy of black holes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:07.380
a measure of how information gets&nbsp;
scrambled or mixed up inside.

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:13.200
Ramanujan's cryptic work still conceals many&nbsp;
mathematical treasures waiting to be discovered.&nbsp;

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:18.780
As Ono put it: “It’s like he was writing down&nbsp;
a bible for us, but it was incomplete. He gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:22.560
us glimpses of what the future would&nbsp;
be, and our job is to figure it out.”

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:25.680
Ramanujan left behind three notebooks packed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:30.120
with his formulas and loose pages&nbsp;
that were only discovered in 1976.

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:36.360
He was still scribbling away four&nbsp;
days before he died on April 26, 1920.

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:39.120
Ramanujan was only 32 years old.

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:44.160
Most of his orthodox relatives stayed away&nbsp;
from his funeral because they considered him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:48.420
tainted for having crossed the waters&nbsp;
to England, and he had been too ill to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:52.200
make it to the purification ceremonies&nbsp;
his mother had arranged at the seaside.

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:57.840
When Hardy was invited to receive an honorary&nbsp;
degree at Harvard, he mentioned in his speech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:02.340
that the most significant achievement&nbsp;
of his life was discovering Ramanujan.

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:09.300
“I did not invent him — like other great&nbsp;
men, he invented himself - but I was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:14.940
first really competent person who had the chance&nbsp;
to see some of his work, and I can still remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:20.040
with satisfaction that I could recognize at&nbsp;
once what a treasure I had found.” (page 207)

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:25.260
“...my association with him is the&nbsp;
one romantic incident in my life.”

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.160
We are left to wonder how many Ramanujans are in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:32.280
India or elsewhere today,&nbsp;
waiting to be discovered.

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:35.340
This isn’t just a story about&nbsp;
a brilliant mathematician.

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:40.020
It’s a story about how educational&nbsp;
institutions can nurture talent and hinder it.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:44.100
Sometimes, the traditional classroom&nbsp;
isn't the best place for learning math.

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:48.000
That's why I trust Brilliant – a website&nbsp;
and app where you can learn math,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:50.460
data science, and computer science interactively.

